1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Kimani Maruhi was an 84-year-old man living in Kenya when he made history.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
In 2004, the Kenyan government announced free primary education for all. For most people,

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
this meant children could finally attend school without burdening their families.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
But for Kimani, it meant something far more personal, a second chance at something he had never had,

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
an education. Despite his age, Kimani walked into a local school wearing a school uniform,

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
and asked to be enrolled in grade one. People laughed. Some questioned why an elderly man would

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
want to sit among six-year-olds learning the alphabet. But Kimani wasn't ashamed. He had never had

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
the chance to learn to read or write as a child. Now, at 84, he was ready to change that. Every day,

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
he attended class, sitting on a small bench, carefully holding a pencil in his wrinkled hands.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
He studied with the determination of someone who knew the value of opportunity.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,000
When others took learning for granted, Kimani cherished it. In just one year, he was elected

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
the head boy of the school, not because of his age, but because of his dedication and positive

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
attitude. His story spread across the world. He was even invited to speak at the United Nations

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
in New York in 2005, becoming the oldest person to start primary school, according to the Guinness

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,000
World Records. Until his death in 2009, Kimani continued to study. His dream was to one day

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
read the Bible on his own.

